# an ending
@title The Weight
@bg field
@mood dread

narrate It worked, and Corin did it alone, and he did it the way he had learned to in five years of ledger-keeping: he wrote it down, and he did not flinch, and he paid the till.
narrate He was twenty-four years old and he had ended it, and by the spring he was the most famous man in the eastern provinces and the most dangerous.
narrate Because a man who can end a plague is a man who can start one, and the Order knew it, and the Order came to him — not with a rope this time, with a pen.
narrate He took it.
narrate He was very good at it. That was the horror of it.
warden neutral "Say the number."
corin neutral "Two hundred."
warden neutral "That's the population. Say the number."
corin neutral "If we don't, it reaches the coast in nineteen days, and the number stops being a number."
narrate Corin signed it.
narrate And then he sat alone in a room with a ledger and read back four pages, and found nothing, and went to bed, and slept perfectly, and did not dream of anything at all.

@ending weight
